<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adal Sultanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Adal, Adalite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Adal Sultanate was a Muslim state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, centred on the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, which flourished from the fifteenth to the sixteenth century. It controlled trade routes linking the Ethiopian interior to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> ibn Ibrahim al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> it invaded and devastated much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, before declining after his death in 1543.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate.jpg" alt="Adal Sultanate" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FDRMRZUSA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Adal_Sultanate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Adal was a general term for a region of lowlands inhabited by Muslims, east of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a>, in the area stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>. In 1288 it was conquered by the Ifat Sultanate, though it kept some independence under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> rule, and in 1332 the Ethiopian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a> invaded it. In 1415 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabr-ad-din-iii">Sabr ad-Din III</a>, eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saad-ad-din-ii">Sa'ad ad-Din II</a>, returned from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> to restore his father's throne. He proclaimed himself king of Adal and made Dakkar his capital. The kingdom was founded on the Harar plateau after the fall of the Sultanate of Ifat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabr-ad-din-iii">Sabr ad-Din III</a>'s brother and successor Mansur ad-Din invaded the royal seat of the Solomonic Empire and drove Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawit-i">Dawit I</a> to Yedaya, where, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a>, he destroyed a Solomonic army and killed the emperor. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badlay">Sultan Badlay</a> the polity controlled territory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-guardafui">Cape Guardafui</a> to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suakin">Suakin</a>. After Badlay died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gomit">Battle of Gomit</a>, his successor Muhammad ibn Badlay submitted to Emperor Baeda Maryam I and paid annual tribute. Adal's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> saw this as a betrayal, and Emir Laday Usman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> seized power in 1471, leaving the sultan a ceremonial post.</p><p>In 1529 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> ibn Ibrahim al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> began the conquest of Abyssinia and won a decisive victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shimbra-kure">Battle of Shimbra Kure</a>. By 1535 he controlled the east and centre of Abyssinia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawit-ii">Emperor Dawit II</a> became a hunted fugitive. In 1559 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid">Nur ibn Mujahid</a>, emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>, invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> and killed Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galawdewos">Gelawdewos</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fatagar">Battle of Fatagar</a>. Sultan Barakat ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-din">Umar Din</a> was then defeated and killed by another Ethiopian army, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma dynasty</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> migrants invaded, Nur's army was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hazalo">Battle of Hazalo</a>, and a famine struck Harar. Nur died in 1567. In 1577 the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aussa">Aussa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Adal had a string of ports and inland cities, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> and Dakkar, with courtyard houses, mosques, shrines and cisterns, integrated into the trade networks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. It traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, fruits, slaves, gold, barley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and its cities imported glass bracelets and celadon ware from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Dynasty</a>. Its people included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somalis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afars</a>, Argobba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Hararis</a>, and Arabic served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> the army was reorganised into three flexible units, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> were used in the conquest of Abyssinia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adal Sultanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He centralised administration by replacing hereditary regional lords with appointed officials, defeated the <b>Adal Sultanate</b> at the Battle of Gomit in 1445, and patronised a golden age of Ge'ez literature.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Haqq ad-Din II's removal of the capital to the Harar plateau makes him for some the true founder of the <b>Adal Sultanate</b>, which emerged with Harar as its capital as the leading Muslim principality of the south-east in the later fifteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-ifat">Sultanate of Ifat · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The forces of Imam Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi of the <b>Adal Sultanate</b> fought the Ethiopians under Emperor Dawit II at Amba Sel, and Adal seized southern Ethiopia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Dara disappears from the record in the 15th century, absorbed into neighbouring provinces and later overrun during the wars of <b>Adal</b> and the Oromo migrations.</blockquote><a href="/state/dara-sultanate">Dara Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Hadiya joined the jihad of Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi of <b>Adal</b> in the 1530s, and was then broken up by the Oromo migrations of the 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/hadiya-sultanate">Hadiya Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Adal</b> leadership moved to the Imamate of Aussa, Harar was left to local governors.</blockquote><a href="/state/harar-emirate">Harar Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
